On Jan 22, 2:16 pm, Eric Miears <ewmie...@gmail.com> wrote:
> I love the idea of a monk, but in practice they are much
> less effective than other combat roles.

Monk is not primarily a combat role.  They can do some
combat, but monks are primarily spellcasters.

> It seems they try to straddle both magic and combat.

A better example of that would be Tourist.  Don't be fooled
by that base spellcasting penalty of 8:  the huge robe
bonus more than makes up for it, and monks are one of only
two roles that *start* with a robe.  Wizards are the only
role that is better for spellcasting than Monks, and
Wizards have practically no combat ability at all (until
the late game, when things are much more equalized from
role to role in general, because you've shored up most
of your weaknesses with all kinds of gear and whatnot).

> Grand Master is no match for for an artifact weapon, and
> I wish it wasn't so.

It's called balance.  No one role should be the best,
or second-best, at both combat and spellcasting.  Monks
are very very close to the best at spellcasting, so
they shouldn't be much better than average at combat.

Note too that if we were being realistic, a martial arts
grandmaster wouldn't do more damage than any character
with moderate skill ("Skilled" I guess that would be in
NetHack parlance) with any half-decent weapon, e.g., a
short sword.  (Of course, NetHack is not real life.
If it were there probably wouldn't be giant ants,
dragons, magic lamps, etc.)  Making a martial arts
grandmaster do damage comparable to an expert with
a (non-artifact) longsword is in fact rather generous.

